\section{Select a Declaration Before Selecting Its Machine Body}

The call \texttt{measure(x)} can involve several distinct
decisions. Name resolution locates an overload set, overload
resolution chooses a declaration, and generic instantiation
supplies arguments to that declaration. If the chosen
declaration is a virtual method, a fourth decision is made
when the receiver is known. Static determination of an
overload is not evidence that the final method body is fixed.
That later selection is the subject of \Cref{ch:ext-404}.
The local OCaml concepts text contrasts Java overloading with
overriding; the Rust book illustrates substitution of concrete
types during monomorphization.

In our toy language a shape printer provides \texttt{encode(Int)},
\texttt{encode(Real)}, and \texttt{encode[T:Scalar](Vector[T])}.
Implicit widening from integer to real is allowed. Exact
matches outrank widening, but comparisons across parameters
use dominance, not a sum: a candidate must be no worse on all
arguments and better on at least one. Use language-defined
tie-breakers only after this comparison. Treat candidates that
cannot infer their required type arguments as inapplicable.

\section{Keep Conversions and Substitutions in the Typed Call}

\Cref{fig:visual-403} splits the information a front end
derives from the callable instance that the back end emits.
An inserted conversion belongs to the selected candidate,
not to the source argument independent of the candidate.
Record it rather than looking at the arguments again after
optimization; a value proven constant later does not entitle
the compiler to choose a different overload.

