When candidate resolution tries inference, its tentative
unifications belong to that attempt. Roll them back before
trying another candidate. Distinguish a failed constraint
from an ambiguous successful pair so that diagnostics can
show exactly where each candidate falls out. Resolve a
selected generic's obligations to the concrete conformance
identities that lowering will need.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Collect Concrete Instances With a Worklist}

\begin{verbatim}
make_typed_call(candidates, operands):
    trials := []
    for c in candidates:
        trial := infer_in_private_context(c, operands)
        if trial.applicable: trials.push(trial)
    best := require_unique_best(trials)
    return Call(best.decl, best.substitution, best.conformances,
                apply_conversions(best.plan, operands))

request(decl, substitution, conformances):
    key := instance_key(decl, substitution, conformances, target)
    if key not in symbols:
        symbols[key] := reserve_symbol(key)
        work.push(key)
    return symbols[key]

while work not empty:
    key := work.pop()
    ir := substitute_checked_body(key)
    for edge in ir.direct_generic_edges:
        edge.symbol := request(edge.decl, edge.args, edge.evidence)
    emit(symbols[key], ir)
\end{verbatim}

Reserving a symbol before visiting its dependencies handles a
recursive call at the same instantiation: \texttt{scan[Int]}
can refer to itself before the body has been emitted. Roots
must include reachable address-taken functions and concrete
instances named as exports, not just ordinary call
instructions. If conformance arguments can vary at one type,
the instance key must distinguish them; even the same data
layout does not imply identical implementations of an operation.

\section{Bound Expansion That Does Not Repeat a Key}

The worklist alone does not terminate an expansion in which a
generic calls itself at strictly growing types. For example,
instantiating \texttt{nest[Int]} may require
\texttt{nest[Vector[Int]]}, which needs another vector layer.
A runtime base case does not necessarily remove the emission
dependency: the compiler may not know which path will run.
Specify a rule that detects unsupported growth or issue a
diagnostic at a bounded compiler resource limit, with a few
links from the instantiation chain. If the language requires
such programs to compile, provide a representation-sharing or
dictionary-passing implementation for the problematic code.

\begin{verbatim}
fn scan[T](xs):
    if xs.empty: return 0
    return 1 + scan[T](xs.tail)       // repeats the same key

fn nest[T](x, n):
    if n == 0: return
    nest[Vector[T]](singleton(x), n - 1)
    // the concrete type keeps growing even if n decreases
\end{verbatim}

Separate compilation gives a further choice: export checked
generic IR and allow consumers to instantiate, or provide
shared generic code with a documented representation contract.
For the former, duplicate concrete bodies can use a linker
deduplication mechanism when available; the build must still
guarantee that all providers of a given key agree, including
which generic body revision was used. Equal symbol spellings do
not repair inconsistent instantiation inputs. Distinguish the
interface dependencies of a client calling a nongeneric routine
from those of a client emitting a library generic's body.

Measure size and runtime separately. Direct calls often enable
inlining and scalar optimizations, but many nearly identical
instances can occupy instruction-cache space and increase build
time. Test both overload choice and emission: a pair
\texttt{(Int,Real)} versus \texttt{(Real,Int)} for two integer
arguments must be ambiguous under the dominance rule; two
calls to one successful vector specialization should request
one emission key. Also add a fresh override without adding an
overload: a virtual caller's descriptor should stay unchanged
even when its observed target changes.

\section{Share Only Code That Has the Same Representation Contract}

Suppose \texttt{Count} and \texttt{Index} are distinct nominal
types represented by the same integer word. A compiler could
emit one machine function for \texttt{length[Count]} and
\texttt{length[Index]} if their representation, generated
operations, and calling conventions agree. It cannot apply the
same rule to \texttt{encode} if the two types supply different
formatters. They can share a body that calls an explicitly
passed formatter, but not a body that baked in either
formatter's address. The compiler is then choosing a different
lowering strategy, not changing the generic's source meaning.

\begin{verbatim}
length[Count] -> length_word
length[Index] -> length_word
encode[Count] -> encode_word(count_format, argument)
encode[Index] -> encode_word(index_format, argument)
\end{verbatim}

To share code, prove compatibility for returns and indirect
arguments as well as scalar parameters. A function copying
values might use different move, copy, or tracing behavior
even when size and alignment match. A language exposing
function identity may also constrain aliasing of public entry
points; small wrappers can preserve distinct source-visible
function identities while delegating to a shared body.

Compare both code generation strategies on two nominal word
types whose formatters produce deliberately different strings.
Then feed the same two types into a generic that just counts
elements and compare emitted body count. Neither test alone
justifies the optimization: the first verifies that sharing
preserves behavior, the second detects whether the compiler
actually eliminated redundancy in a case where it could.

Also compare builds with and without link-time optimization.
A merge performed at link time must preserve the same evidence
and public entry-point distinctions as a merge in the front end.
